+Checklist of common mistakes to check your solution against before finishing:
+- Off-by-one errors: check loop bounds, inclusive vs. exclusive ranges, 0-based vs. 1-based indexing, and fence-post cases (e.g. number of segments vs. number of points).
+- Smallest inputs: verify behavior for n = 0 or 1, empty input, single elements, and minimal values allowed by the constraints.
+- Largest inputs: verify behavior at the maximum constraints, check for time-complexity problems (e.g. O(n^2) when n is large), and watch for integer overflow concerns or huge intermediate values.
+- Integer division: use `//` where integer results are required, be careful with `/` (which yields floats in Python), and check rounding direction for negative numbers (`//` floors toward negative infinity).
+- Input/output format: match the exact output format (spacing, newlines, case of YES/NO, etc.) and make sure all test cases in the input are processed.
+
 Additional guidelines for competitive programming solutions:
 - Reading input efficiently: when the program reads standard input, read all of it at once (e.g. `data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()` or `sys.stdin.read().split()`) and consume tokens with an index, instead of calling `input()` once per line. This is much faster for large inputs. Make sure to handle empty lines and trailing whitespace correctly.
 - Avoiding recursion-depth limits: Python's default recursion limit (about 1000) can be exceeded by recursive DFS, tree traversal, or divide-and-conquer on large inputs. Prefer iterative implementations (explicit stacks or queues). If recursion is unavoidable, call `sys.setrecursionlimit(...)` with a sufficiently large value and consider running the solution in a `threading.Thread` with an increased `threading.stack_size(...)`.
